Kent Paul Dolan <xanthian@well.com> wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
>
>> I'd like to spice up NetHack-De a little bit, but
>> without actually changing the game play in a
>> drastic way. That means, no SporkHack-De or
>> Slash'Em-De (for the time being :).
>
>> Essentially just fixing the most glaring bugs or
>> shortcomings of NetHack 3.4.3.
>
> If I may suggest, even if what you are doing is
> "just a bugfix", fork the code so that those who
> want to play exactly "vanilla nethack" (say, to
> compete on a fair basis with others), but in their
> own language, have that choice, while those who want
> to play "vanilla nethack -- bugfixed" in German,
> have that choice.

I will at least put the patches into the repository so that anybody
with the ability to compile NetHack can reverse apply the patches to
get a vanilla version. For the time being I think I won't release a
binary version of NetHack-De, but if there's demand for a vanilla
version I will certainly reconsider.

Another question is which distribution are still distributing a
vanilla NetHack?

Debian doesn't, for Gentoo there's a possibility to use Jukkas
combined patch and even NAO doesn't use a completely vanilla version
(http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Nethack.alt.org).

This is of course an effect of almost five years without even one
proper bugfix release. In the meantime there has been a lot of
inofficial patches that enhance and/or fix the game and my guess is
that there are way more players out there that don't play a vanilla
version. Just think about those windows user we've seen that have
were desperately trying to compile NetHack on Windows.

> And under "glaring shortcomings", please consider
> the difficult task of making bigger levels (among
> the ones that are "randomly generated") possible
> just by dimension settings in the startup options
> file. I'd really like to play NetHack on a much
> bigger level, 80x24 get boring fairly fast.

I'm not sure if NetHacks level algorithm is suited for larger maps.

I changed COLNO and ROWNO from 80x21 to 100x35 in include/global.h and
looked at the first level:
http://bhaak.dyndns.org/nethack/nethack_100x35.png

Especially the random corridors are not really nicely put.

> [Also,
> to make this work easily, I'd like to play with the
> much nicer Crawl "local and global level display"
> interface, while I'm wishing for the moon.]

What does it do?

> This task is "very difficult" because, while the
> dimension sizes are already parameterized in the
> game, cripplingly many places that they should be
> checked in a calculation, it seems that they are
> not, and finding all those flaws would require
> pretty much a preen of the entire source code base.

Wouldn't also object creation have to rebalanced? Otherwise you'd get
a lot more objects just because there are more rooms that get random
objects.

